\ Genuine variable-arity words (declared `| f`), the shape of lib/lexer.1z's
\ rule match words: an `if` whose two arms leave different concrete depths --
\ two values on a "match", one `f` on a "no-match" -- built from fixed-output
\ natives only, so the variability is structural rather than an artifact of a
\ mis-modeled variable-arity callee. The `if` merge collapses the concrete
\ unequal arms to a runtime row the caller reads, instead of rejecting them as
\ a stack-shape mismatch (the old NC.13). Each word is compiled standalone (via
\ quotation dispatch) and the program runs interpreter-free.

\ Flat: a single `if` whose arms leave 2 (match) and 1 (no-match).
classify-flat: ( n -- a b | f ) [ dup 0 > [ dup 100 + ] [ drop f ] if ] ;

\ Nested (the lexer shape): an outer guard `if` whose false arm holds the inner
\ unequal `if`. The inner if's row must propagate out through the outer merge.
classify-nested: ( n -- a b | f ) [
  dup 5 < [ drop f ] [
    dup 10 > [ dup 100 + ] [ drop f ] if
  ] if
] ;

\ Consumers dispatch the variable-arity word via a quotation (tail position) and
\ branch on the row result, mirroring lib/lexer.1z's (try-rules). Distinguishing
\ `f` (depth 1) from a two-value match requires the row's runtime depth to be
\ correct, so the branch taken is itself the assertion that the arity varied.
read-flat: ( n -- s ) [ [ classify-flat ] call dup f = [ drop "no" ] [ drop drop "match" ] if ] ;
read-nested: ( n -- s ) [ [ classify-nested ] call dup f = [ drop "no" ] [ drop drop "match" ] if ] ;

5 read-flat print-line
0 read-flat print-line
3 read-nested print-line
7 read-nested print-line
50 read-nested print-line
